  "output": "I'll verify the file's current content and check which validation tools are available, then run an OGC validity check.File confirmed. Shapely isn't installed — I'll try installing it for a GEOS-backed validation (the reference implementation of OGC validity).The venv is read-only. Trying an install to a writable target directory instead.Shapely 2.2.0 installed. Now running the full structural and OGC validity analysis.Validation complete. Here is the report:\n\n## Validity status: INVALID ❌\n\n**File:** [invalid_polygon.geojson](/home/agent/workspace/invalid_polygon.geojson) (verified, unmodified)\n\n**Structure check — passes.** The file parses as valid JSON and is structurally correct GeoJSON: a `FeatureCollection` → `Feature` → `Polygon`, with a closed exterior ring (first point equals last point, `[0,0]`).\n\n**Geometry check — fails OGC Simple Features.** Validated with Shapely 2.2.0 (GEOS, the reference implementation used by PostGIS/JTS):\n\n- `is_valid` → `False`\n- `explain_validity` → **`Ring Self-intersection[0 0]`**\n- Ring simplicity (`is_simple`) → `False`\n\n### Reason and location\n\nThe exterior ring coordinates are:\n\n```\n[0,0] → [2,0] → [2,2] → [0,2] → [0,0] → [1,1] → [0,0]\n                                ↑ idx 4          ↑ idx 6\n```\n\nThe ring arrives at its closing point `(0,0)` at **vertex index 4**, but instead of ending, it continues on a detour to `(1,1)` and returns to `(0,0)` at index 6. This produces two violations of OGC SFS ring-simplicity rules:\n\n1. **Self-intersection at point (0,0)** — the vertex `(0,0)` is visited three times (indices 0, 4, and 6), so non-adjacent boundary segments meet there (e.g., segment `[0,0]→[2,0]` crosses segments `[0,2]→[0,0]` and `[0,0]→[1,1]`). A ring must not touch or cross itself except at its single closure point. This is the location GEOS reports.\n2. **Degenerate spike along segment (0,0)–(1,1)** — the outgoing segment `[0,0]→[1,1]` (indices 4–5) is immediately retraced by `[1,1]→[0,0]` (indices 5–6), so the boundary traverses the diagonal twice. The spike encloses zero area and cuts through the interior of the 2×2 square, so the boundary no longer cleanly separates a connected interior from the exterior.\n\n### Minimal fix (not applied — the file was only inspected)\n\nTruncate the ring to the simple closed square: `[[0,0],[2,0],[2,2],[0,2],[0,0]]` — i.e., drop the `[1,1]` vertex and the duplicate closure. Say the word if you'd like me to apply it.",
